WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:09.018
Public-key cryptography allows parties to communicate or verify trust without first sharing a secret key through a private channel.

2
00:00:09.018 --> 00:00:14.205
RSA, Diffie-Hellman, and elliptic curve cryptography are common examples.

3
00:00:14.205 --> 00:00:27.293
They appear in key exchange, certificates, digital signatures, secure shell access, VPNs, TLS, identity systems, code signing, firmware signing, and many forms of public key infrastructure.

4
00:00:27.293 --> 00:00:32.799
The risk is serious because these mechanisms protect more than confidentiality.

5
00:00:32.799 --> 00:00:38.306
Key exchange helps parties establish session keys for encrypted communication.

6
00:00:38.306 --> 00:00:42.775
Digital signatures support integrity, authenticity, and trust.

7
00:00:42.775 --> 00:00:45.887
Certificates bind identities to public keys.

8
00:00:45.887 --> 00:00:49.558
PKI connects those certificates into chains of trust.

9
00:00:49.558 --> 00:00:59.534
If public-key assumptions fail, the impact can reach authentication, software supply chain trust, device identity, and secure update workflows.

10
00:00:59.534 --> 00:01:06.237
TLS, VPNs, and SSH are easy examples because they are visible to administrators and security teams.

11
00:01:06.237 --> 00:01:18.846
But public-key cryptography also appears inside application frameworks, service meshes, cloud control planes, package managers, mobile apps, hardware devices, and vendor products.

12
00:01:18.846 --> 00:01:27.306
Many teams do not have a complete map of these dependencies, which is why cryptographic inventory is a foundational activity.

13
00:01:27.306 --> 00:01:31.535
This course avoids instructions for attacking cryptography.

14
00:01:31.535 --> 00:01:34.807
The practitioner focus is defensive readiness.

15
00:01:34.807 --> 00:01:54.359
The useful questions are where public-key cryptography exists, what data or trust depends on it, who controls the implementation, how long it must remain secure, and what would be required to migrate it without breaking availability, interoperability, compliance, or operational trust.
